<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Udai Singh II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Udai Singh II of Mewar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Udai Singh II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> of Mewar until his death in 1572 and the founder of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> in 1559. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a>, he escaped as a child from the usurper Banvir, who had killed his brother Vikramaditya, and later won back the throne. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> besieged and took Chittor in 1567–1568, he withdrew to the hills, and Udaipur became Mewar's new capital. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> continued the resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii.jpg" alt="Udai Singh II" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tanvi12 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rana-udai-singh-ii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Udai Singh was born in Chittor on 4 August 1522, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rani-karnavati">Rani Karnavati</a>, a princess of Bundi. After his father's death his brother Ratan Singh II became king and was assassinated in 1531, and another brother, Vikramaditya Singh, succeeded. When Bahadur Shah, the Muzaffarid Sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, sacked Chittor in 1535, Udai Singh was sent to Bundi for safety. When Banvir seized the throne in 1537 the boy's nurse Panna Dai sacrificed her own son Chandan to save him and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a>, where he lived for two years disguised as a nephew of the governor Asha Shah Depura.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The nobles of Mewar crowned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a> in 1540, the year his eldest son Pratap was born. Weakened by the civil war, he surrendered Chittor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> in 1544 on condition that the people of Mewar were not harmed. In 1557 Maldev Rathore defeated him at the Battle of Harmada and took Merta, and in 1562 he gave refuge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baz-bahadur">Baz Bahadur</a>, the last ruler of the Malwa Sultanate, whose kingdom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> had annexed.</p><p>Judging Chittor too exposed, Udai Singh and his council planned a safer capital, and in 1559 work began in the Girwa part of Mewar, with an artificial lake for cultivation completed in 1562. Warned by his son Shakti Singh in September 1567 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s plan, he took his council's advice and withdrew with the princes to the hills at Gogunda, leaving Chittor to his chieftains Rao Jaimal and Patta. Akbar took the fort on 23 February 1568 after a siege of four months and the sack that followed killed the garrison and 25-40,000 civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Udai Singh died at Gogunda on 28 February 1572. He had 24 sons by several wives, and his favourite wife, Dheer Bai Bhattiyani of Jaisalmer, was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagmal-singh">Jagmal Singh</a>, who tried to take the throne. The nobles of Mewar stopped him and crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> on 1 March 1572.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Udai Singh II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Successive rulers built some of the largest reservoirs of pre-modern India: Lake Pichola was enlarged when <b>Udai Singh II</b> founded Udaipur, Raj Singh I created Rajsamand Lake in the 1660s and 1670s, and Jai Singh built Jaisamand Lake in the late seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chittorgarh, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Gogunda, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.89,74.62&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Chittorgarh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6530 1248 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Udai Singh II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6980 1518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6980 1518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.89,74.62&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
